Ethridge: the role of a conceptual framework
For Ethridge, a conceptual framework is an analysis of a specific research problem using relevant theory and concepts (Ethridge 2004).
It should:

explain the relationships that produce the problem;
identify relevant variables and conditions;
state maintained assumptions;
produce hypotheses that follow from the reasoning; and
clarify the scope of the conclusions.

A review of standard theory alone is not a conceptual framework. The theory must be applied to the problem under study.

Apply Ethridge: define the problem precisely
“Food access” can refer to different outcomes:

the number and location of SNAP-authorized retailers;
distance or travel time to a retailer;
the assortment of qualifying foods inside a store;
prices and product quality;
reliability of availability over time; or
household purchases and diet quality.

The framework must state which outcome is being explained. These outcomes need not move in the same direction.

What Ethridge prevents us from assuming
The following chain does not follow by definition:
more required varieties⇒more retailer participation⇒better household access⇒better diets.
\text{more required varieties}
\Rightarrow \text{more retailer participation}
\Rightarrow \text{better household access}
\Rightarrow \text{better diets}.

Possible countervailing responses include:

a store carries more qualifying products but reduces other inventory;
compliance raises costs or prices;
a retailer gives up SNAP authorization;
households substitute to another store or shop less often; and
formal compliance does not imply consistent in-stock availability.

Which assumptions produce those results?
The simple model assumes:

the requirement is enforced and retailers understand it;
rr restricts the feasible set for authorized retailers;
revenue and cost functions are otherwise fixed when rr changes;
retailers can choose whether to remain authorized; and
the relevant alternative is summarized by ViNV_i^N.

If the rule also changes consumer demand, enforcement costs, competitors’ behavior, or eligibility through another criterion, the model needs another margin.
This is Varian’s simplify–evaluate–generalize sequence.

From a policy rationale to a research problem
The policy rationale contains several links
stocking requirement→retailer inventory→local availability
\text{stocking requirement}
\rightarrow \text{retailer inventory}
\rightarrow \text{local availability}
 →household purchases→diet and health
\rightarrow \text{household purchases}
\rightarrow \text{diet and health}

A conceptual framework explains each link and identifies the conditions needed for it to hold.
Retailer participation and compliance costs may also shape the path from the requirement to local availability.

Translating the policy issue into a research problem
Intended stocking pathway
stocking requirement→retailer inventory→local availability→purchases and health
\text{stocking requirement}
\rightarrow \text{retailer inventory}
\rightarrow \text{local availability}
\rightarrow \text{purchases and health}


Retailer participation pathway
stocking requirement→compliance cost→SNAP authorization→authorized-store portfolio
\text{stocking requirement}
\rightarrow \text{compliance cost}
\rightarrow \text{SNAP authorization}
\rightarrow \text{authorized-store portfolio}


For purposes of this example, the application observes the second pathway.

Inventory remains a theoretical mechanism rather than an observed outcome.

Data quality begins before the questionnaire
Champ treats high-quality data as a prerequisite for reliable and valid nonmarket values (Champ 2017)
The research objective determines
whose values matter→who must be represented→how they can be reached
\text{whose values matter}
\rightarrow
\text{who must be represented}
\rightarrow
\text{how they can be reached}

A well-worded questionnaire cannot repair a frame that excludes part of the relevant population

What happens when R calls an API
R function→request→data service→response→R object
\text{R function}
\longrightarrow
\text{request}
\longrightarrow
\text{data service}
\longrightarrow
\text{response}
\longrightarrow
\text{R object}


Endpoint is the web address for a data service
Parameters specify the place, time, variables, and other selections
Response is the information returned by the service
R package translates the response into a familiar object

Many services transmit a structured text format called JSON. The packages in today’s examples read that format and return a data frame, map, or raster
